  "id": "Compound::DB00703 - Disease::DOID:1686",
  "verbalization": "Methazolamide is implicated in the pathogenesis of glaucoma through multiple indirect pathways, with the most direct link emerging through its role in causing myopia, which in turn is treated by dorzolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor. This pathway highlights a potential mechanism by which Methazolamide may influence ocular pressure, indirectly contributing to glaucoma. The shared lowest common ancestor between Methazolamide and dorzolamide, both belonging to the class of organic heterocyclic compounds, suggests a common biochemical pathway or structural feature that may underlie their respective effects. Additionally, the involvement of dorzolamide in treating glaucoma reinforces the significance of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in this context. While the evidence is indirect, the cumulative effect of these relationships points to a possible role of Methazolamide in glaucoma through its influence on ocular physiology, mediated by dorzolamide and its class of drugs.",
